{"chunk_id":"R3-257103-001","collection":"ran3_tdoc_chunks","metadata":{"source":["Nokia"],"tdocNumber":"R3-257103","title":"(TP to TR 38.745) Multi-hop UE trajectory across gNBs"},"point_id":"2308434989866459620"}
Rel-20 NG-RAN AI/ML study focuses on AI/ML optimization of aspects pertaining to AI/ML mobility. In particular, one of the objectives is to study multi-hop UE trajectory across gNBs. One of the key enablers of AI/ML for NG-RAN in Rel-18 is related to UE Trajectory. Predicted UE Trajectory information may be sent in Rel-18 from a source node to a target node during a handover. Correspondingly, a source node may request measured UE Trajectory information from a target node for a handed over UE. The measured UE Trajectory is cell-based and is measured over the cells of the target node. This enables the source node predict UE Trajectory over the target node cells, but also to monitor the performance of its prediction. One of the fundamental limitations of Rel-18 predicted (single-hop) trajectory is that it is sent to a target node at a time of a UE handover. So, when the trajectory is sent to the target node the UE, for which the trajectory is provided, is already known. This means that even though a source node can use its calculated predicted trajectory to determine the best target cell, the node receiving predicted trajectory has limited possibilities to use the predicted trajectory information for its own resource optimization, e.g., limited to intra-gNB subsequent handovers and resource reservation. Another limitation of this approach is that the trajectory is, optionally, sent to the target node without the target having to request for it. The assumption for this design was that predicted trajectory information served the role of “assistance information” provided to the target node of a handover, if this information is available at the source. Finally, even though different UE Trajectory granularities were considered, based on cell level, beam level, or detailed location level, in Rel-18 we finally considered only cell level predicted and measured UE trajectory. This decision was driven by the considered use cases which only involved cell level decisions. In the rest of this paper, we provide our views for various aspects related to multi-hop UE Trajectory that could be studied in Rel-20.

